4.Test the login or registration flow of any installed app on both your emulator and a real Android phone (if available). Compare the experience and list at least two differences you notice in speed, UI, or notifications.
Answer :

I tested the login/registration flow of the installed app on both the Android Emulator and a real Android phone.

The login page was opened, login details were entered, and the login button was tested on both devices.

Differences observed:
1)Speed: The app loaded and responded slightly faster on the real Android phone, while the emulator took a little longer to open screens and process the login.
2)UI: The UI looked almost the same on both devices, but the real phone had smoother scrolling and touch responses.
3)Notifications: Notifications appeared normally on the real phone, while notifications on the emulator depended on the emulator's notification and internet settings.



5.Pick any app you use daily (Instagram, Zomato, Paytm, etc.) and describe how you would test push notifications using both an emulator and a real device. Mention at least one limitation of emulators for this test.
Answer :

First, I would log in to Instagram on both the Android Emulator and a real Android phone. Then I would enable notification permissions and trigger activities such as receiving a new message, a like, or a comment.

On the emulator, I would check whether the notification appears correctly, whether the notification sound/vibration works, and whether tapping the notification opens the correct Instagram screen.

On the real device, I would perform the same tests and check the notification when the phone is locked, when Instagram is running in the background, and when the app is completely closed.

>>Limitation of Emulator: Push notifications and background behavior may not always work exactly like a real phone because the emulator may have limitations with Google Play services, network connectivity, background processing, sound, and vibration
